Hi Luca,

It is great that you are using the BNP function in nimble! I’m sorry you are having issued though.

It could be that the dimension of the categorical distribution is causing the problem. I would have to take a closer look. Could you please share a code that I can run?

I am not sure how to bypass this using the dCRP function, maybe Chris has an idea?

One alternative is to use the stick-breaking representation for the random measure. See this post {https://www.r-bloggers.com/2018/12/bayesian-nonparametric-models-in-nimble-part-1-density-estimation/} for an example illustrating its usage. In that case you get the posterior samples from the random measure directly, so I think it should be fine.

I think you were wondering about mixing… I have used the dCRP and the stick-breaking in nimble for different models. I have seen that for more complicated models, the stick-breaking function mixes better than the dCRP. The MCMC is definitely slower for the stick-breaking, but I think it is worth trying it.

I hope this helps.

Please let me know if you have any questions or if something is not clear.
Best,
Claudia

