Hi Luca,

Thanks for the extra details. So, each data vector is modeled as a product of normal and categorical kernels.

I see the convenience in doing this to find whatever marginal or conditional you might be interested in.

I agree that using dCRP rather than the stick-breaking seems “more nonparametric”. I do think you will get very similar results with both, even when truncating the random measure. And also, in real life you will always have a finite number of clusters. I wish we could live in a theoretical world :) Just a note: even when using the dCRP you will never get more clusters than data points you have.

Now, back to your code: there is an issue with using the getSamplesDPmeasure() function for “non standard” models like this one. We will look into this and hopefully find a way so it works.
Meanwhile, I found a workaround that you can use. Basically, you need to write a user defined function for your product of kernels distribution and you need to define the data as a matrix. First two columns are for the real variables and the last 2 are for the categorical variables. I wrote a code, please see it attached, based on what you had (I did some changes though). I included the categorical variables. I was able to run the MCMC and use the getSamplesDPmeasure function. I did not try it for other data sets, but I think it should work fine.

Please, let me know if you have any questions.
I hope this helps.

Best,
Claudia

